---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/docs/Callout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../site';

const TOC = [
  { id: 'precedence', label: 'Precedence' },
  { id: 'environment-variables', label: 'Environment variables' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'Limits' },
  { id: 'access', label: 'Web-UI access' },
  { id: 'providers', label: 'Providers' },
  { id: 'plugins-and-mcp', label: 'Plugins and MCP' },
  { id: 'approvals', label: 'Approvals' },
];

const ENV = [
  {
    names: ['KARUCODE_HOST', 'KARUCODE_PORT'],
    def: '127.0.0.1 / 8787',
    meaning: 'bind address; a non-loopback bind needs a token or sign-in',
  },
  { names: ['KARUCODE_WORKSPACE'], def: 'cwd', meaning: 'default workspace root' },
  {
    names: ['KARUCODE_DATA_DIR'],
    def: '~/.local/share/karucode',
    meaning: 'SQLite database + plugin data, mode 0700',
  },
  {
    names: ['KARUCODE_AUTH_TOKEN'],
    def: 'unset',
    meaning: 'bearer token for remote access (≥16 chars)',
  },
  {
    names: ['KARUCODE_ADMIN_TOKEN'],
    def: 'unset',
    meaning: 'required to change approvals or the endpoint',
  },
  {
    names: ['KARUCODE_PROJECT_ROOTS'],
    def: 'unset (any dir)',
    meaning: 'colon-separated allowlist for new project roots',
  },
  {
    names: ['KARUCODE_ALLOW_TEMP_DIR'],
    def: 'true',
    meaning: 'let file tools also reach the system temp dir',
  },
  {
    names: ['KARUCODE_BASE_URL'],
    def: 'http://127.0.0.1:4000/v1',
    meaning: 'OpenAI-compatible base URL',
  },
  {
    names: ['KARUCODE_API_KEY'],
    def: 'unset',
    meaning: 'provider key (falls back to OPENAI_API_KEY)',
  },
  { names: ['KARUCODE_MODEL'], def: 'gpt-4o-mini', meaning: 'model id' },
  {
    names: ['KARUCODE_APPROVAL_MODE'],
    def: 'ask',
    meaning: 'ask, ask_commands, auto, read_only',
  },
  {
    names: ['KARUCODE_ALLOW_COMMANDS'],
    def: 'empty',
    meaning: 'command prefixes that are never prompted for',
  },
  { names: ['KARUCODE_MAX_ITERATIONS'], def: '0', meaning: 'turns per run; 0 = no ceiling' },
  { names: ['KARUCODE_MAX_RUN_SECS'], def: '86400', meaning: 'hard wall clock for a whole run' },
  {
    names: ['KARUCODE_PARALLEL_TOOL_CALLS'],
    def: 'true',
    meaning: 'run independent tool calls at once',
  },
  { names: ['KARUCODE_COMMAND_TIMEOUT_SECS'], def: '120', meaning: 'default shell timeout' },
  {
    names: ['KARUCODE_MAX_TOOL_OUTPUT_BYTES'],
    def: '24000',
    meaning: 'tool result bytes kept for the model',
  },
  {
    names: ['KARUCODE_CONTEXT_MAX_CHARS'],
    def: '0',
    meaning: '0 = derive from the model’s window',
  },
  {
    names: ['KARUCODE_SUBAGENT_CONCURRENCY'],
    def: '3',
    meaning: 'concurrent subagents (0 = no ceiling)',
  },
  {
    names: ['KARUCODE_SUBAGENT_MAX_BACKGROUND'],
    def: '8',
    meaning: 'live detached agents per session',
  },
  {
    names: ['KARUCODE_ALLOW_RECURSIVE_SUBAGENTS'],
    def: 'false',
    meaning: 'nested delegation',
  },
  {
    names: ['KARUCODE_LOG'],
    def: 'info',
    meaning: 'tracing filter, e.g. info,karucode=debug',
  },
  {
    names: ['KARUCODE_ALLOWED_HOSTS'],
    def: 'empty',
    meaning: 'extra Host names for the loopback guard',
  },
  {
    names: ['KARUCODE_ACCESS_ENABLED'],
    def: 'false',
    meaning: 'require a password or passkey to open the UI',
  },
  {
    names: ['KARUCODE_PUBLIC_URL'],
    def: 'unset',
    meaning: 'the address people type; added to the Host allowlist',
  },
  { names: ['KARUCODE_SESSION_DAYS'], def: '30', meaning: 'how long a browser stays signed in' },
  {
    names: ['KARUCODE_TUNNEL'],
    def: 'none',
    meaning: 'none, cloudflare or custom — which proxy is believed',
  },
];

const MODES = [
  { mode: 'ask', meaning: 'Shell commands and file writes wait for you. The default.' },
  { mode: 'ask_commands', meaning: 'Shell commands wait; file writes do not.' },
  {
    mode: 'auto',
    meaning: 'Nothing waits. Only for a workspace you would let the model change unattended.',
  },
  { mode: 'read_only', meaning: 'No shell commands and no writes.' },
];
---

<Docs
  title="Configuration"
  description="How karucode resolves settings: precedence, environment variables, the TOML config file, limits, providers, plugins, MCP and approvals."
  path="/docs/configuration/"
  section="Docs"
  lede="Four layers of settings, and the keys that actually matter."
  toc={TOC}
  edit={SITE.repo}
  prev={{ href: '/docs/quickstart/', title: 'Quick start' }}
  next={{ href: '/security/', title: 'Security' }}
>
  <h2 id="precedence">Precedence</h2>
  <p>
    Every setting can arrive four ways. When two of them disagree, the higher one wins, and the order
    is fixed:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Overrides saved from the UI, in the SQLite <code>settings</code> table.</li>
    <li>Environment variables.</li>
    <li>
      The config file at <code>KARUCODE_CONFIG</code> — default
      <code>~/.config/karucode/config.toml</code>.
    </li>
    <li>Built-in defaults.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The first layer is the one that surprises people: a value you changed in the browser keeps
    beating the environment on the next start, because it is stored in the database, and the
    database outranks the environment.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="note" title="KARUCODE_LOCK_SETTINGS=1">
    <p>
      Setting this removes layer 1 entirely: the UI stops writing settings and browser attempts to
      change them are rejected with <code>403</code>. The environment and the config file become the
      only sources, which is what you want when a deployment is managed by something else.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="environment-variables">Environment variables</h2>
  <p>
    The table is the reference. Anything not listed here still has a config-file equivalent or a
    flag on the binary — <code>--help</code> lists the flags.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap kc-env">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Variable</th>
          <th scope="col">Default</th>
          <th scope="col">Meaning</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          ENV.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                {row.names.map((name, i) => (
                  <>
                    <code>{name}</code>
                    {i < row.names.length - 1 ? <span class="kc-sep"> / </span> : null}
                  </>
                ))}
              </td>
              <td class="kc-def">{row.def}</td>
              <td>{row.meaning}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
  <p>
    A run is not cut off by a turn counter. <code>max_iterations</code> defaults to
    <code>0</code> — no ceiling — so a long task is not interrupted and no continuation turn is
    injected into the transcript to restart it:
  </p>
  <pre class="code">{`[limits]
max_iterations = 0        # 0 = no turn ceiling (the default)
max_run_secs = 86400      # hard wall clock for the whole run, 24 h
parallel_tool_calls = true`}</pre>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>max_iterations</code> — turns allowed in one run. <code>0</code> means the run ends when
      the work is done, when you cancel it, at the wall clock, or when the loop guard catches it
      re-issuing calls with no workspace change. A non-zero value is a blunt backstop: the run stops
      with <code>limit_reached</code> and says so.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>max_run_secs</code> — a hard wall clock for the whole run. <code>86400</code> is 24
      hours.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>parallel_tool_calls</code> — independent calls (reads, searches, disjoint writes) run at
      once. Anything that can see a sibling’s effects — shell, plugins, MCP, subagents, approvals —
      still runs in call order, and results are folded into the transcript in call order either way.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    There is no segment and no continuation: a long task is one long task. The wall clock, the
    cancel button and the loop guard are what end a run.
  </p>

  <h2 id="access">Web-UI access</h2>
  <p>
    Sign-in is a password or a passkey, exchanged for an <code>HttpOnly</code> session cookie. The
    <em>credentials</em> are not configuration — the password hash and passkeys live in the SQLite
    settings table and are managed from Settings → Access or the first-run wizard — so
    <code>[access]</code> holds only the policy:
  </p>
  <pre class="code">{`[access]
enabled = true                               # require sign-in; default false
public_url = "https://karucode.example.com"  # added to the Host allowlist
session_days = 30                            # 1..=365
tunnel = "cloudflare"                        # none | cloudflare | custom
trusted_proxies = ["10.0.0.0/8"]             # whose forwarded headers are believed`}</pre>
  <p>
    Creating the first credential turns sign-in on, so setting a password is the whole switch.
    <code>enabled = true</code> with no credential is refused: that would leave a login screen nobody
    can pass. There is deliberately no environment variable for the password — a hash in a unit file
    ends up in <code>systemctl show</code>. <a href="/security/">Security</a> covers the session and
    passkey rules.
  </p>

  <h2 id="providers">Providers</h2>
  <p>
    A provider is a name, a base URL and the key to send. Named profiles let you keep several and
    switch the active one without editing the environment:
  </p>
  <pre class="code">{`active_provider = "gateway"
active_model = "gpt-4o-mini"

[[providers]]
id = "gateway"
name = "Local gateway"
provider = "openai"
base_url = "http://127.0.0.1:4000/v1"
api_key = "sk-..."
request_timeout_secs = 600`}</pre>
  <p>
    <code>base_url</code> is a prefix — karucode appends <code>/chat/completions</code>. Providers are
    install-wide, not per project; a <em>session</em> can pin its own provider, model and reasoning
    effort, and that pin travels with the session rather than the checkout.
  </p>

  <h2 id="plugins-and-mcp">Plugins and MCP</h2>
  <p>
    Both are separate processes. A plugin speaks versioned JSON-RPC over stdio; an MCP server speaks
    MCP over stdio or streamable HTTP. Declared, they look like this:
  </p>
  <pre class="code">{`[[plugins]]
name = "background-terminals"
command = "kc-plugin-terminals"
args = ["--stdio"]

[[mcp_servers]]
name = "kg"
command = "kg-mcp"
args = ["--kb", "karucode"]
transport = "stdio"`}</pre>
  <p>
    Tools are namespaced so nothing can shadow a built-in: <code>plugin__&lt;name&gt;__&lt;tool&gt;</code>
    and <code>mcp__&lt;server&gt;__&lt;tool&gt;</code>. MCP surfaces tools only — resources and
    prompts are not exposed — and the legacy SSE-only transport is not implemented.
  </p>
  <p>
    Neither kind is sandboxed. A plugin runs with your permissions. A rebuilt plugin is picked up by
    a reload rather than a restart: <code>POST /api/plugins/reload</code> rescans,
    <code>POST /api/plugins/:name/reload</code> replaces one process, and <code>plugins_watch</code>
    (off by default) does it on a timer. Read the code before you add it, and see
    <a href="/security/">Security</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="approvals">Approvals</h2>
  <p>
    One variable picks the gate: <code>KARUCODE_APPROVAL_MODE</code>. It decides what the agent must
    ask for before it acts.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Mode</th>
          <th scope="col">What happens</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          MODES.map((m) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{m.mode}</td>
              <td>{m.meaning}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Two more keys change the edges of that gate. <code>KARUCODE_ALLOW_COMMANDS</code> is a list of
    command prefixes that are never prompted for — <code>cargo test</code> is the obvious entry.
    <code>KARUCODE_ADMIN_TOKEN</code> is required to change approvals or the endpoint at all, so a
    session token cannot widen its own permissions.
  </p>
  <p>
    Approvals are a gate, not isolation: an approved command runs as the server user with no sandbox
    around it. <code>auto</code> mode is only safe for a workspace you would hand to the model
    directly.
  </p>
</Docs>

<style>
  .kc-env td {
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .kc-sep {
    color: var(--text-3);
  }

  .kc-def {
    font-family: var(--kc-font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--text);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .kc-def {
      white-space: normal;
    }
  }

  /* 23 rows in three columns is unreadable at phone width if two thirds of it
     hide behind a horizontal scroll, so the reference table stacks instead. */
  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .kc-env thead {
      display: none;
    }

    .kc-env table,
    .kc-env tbody,
    .kc-env tr,
    .kc-env td {
      display: block;
      width: 100%;
    }

    .kc-env tr {
      padding: 13px 14px;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-soft);
    }

    .kc-env tr:last-child {
      border-bottom: 0;
    }

    .kc-env td {
      border: 0;
      padding: 0;
      white-space: normal;
    }

    .kc-env td.kc-def {
      margin-top: 3px;
    }

    .kc-env td.kc-def::before {
      content: 'default';
      margin-right: 8px;
      font-family: var(--kc-font-mono);
      font-size: var(--fs-micro);
      letter-spacing: var(--tracking-label);
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--text-3);
    }

    .kc-env td:last-child {
      margin-top: 5px;
      font-size: var(--fs-small);
    }
  }
</style>